; # A Mathematician's Apology. ... 1940.\n\n# A Mathematician's Apology. By G. H. Hardy. (London: Cambridge University Press. 1940. Pp. vii + 93. Price 3s. 6d.)\n\n## Review products\n\nA Mathematician's Apology. By G. H. Hardy. (London: Cambridge University Press. 1940. Pp. vii + 93. Price 3s. 6d.)\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  25 February 2009\n\nC. D. Broad\n\n* * *\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn abstract is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.\n\nImage: Image of the first page of this content. For PDF version, please use the ‘Save PDF’ preceeding this image.'\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    New Books\n\nInformation\n    \n\nPhilosophy , Volume 16 , Issue 63 , July 1941 , pp. 323 - 326\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0031819100002655 [Opens in a new window]\n\nCopyright\n    \n\nCopyright © The Royal Institute of Philosophy 1941\n\n\nA Mathematician's Apology. By G. H. Hardy. (London: Cambridge University Press. 1940. Pp. vii + 93. Price 3s. 6d.)\n\n  * Volume 16, Issue 63\n  * C. D. Broad\n  * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0031819100002655\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch (https://www.cambridge.org/core/search?eventCode=SE-AU&filters%5BauthorTerms%5D=C.+D.+Broad.)\nciteturn26search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago;   * ### A Mathematician's Apology. ... 1940. ...     Philosophy / Volume 15 / Issue 59 / July 1940\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n    * Export citation\n  * View extract\n\nI have been asked to speak to-day on the ‘new philosophy’ which arose and gradually triumphed in the period between the birth of Bruno in 1548 and the death of Descartes in 1650. I propose to put a fairly wide interpretation on the word ‘philosophy’, as did all the great thinkers of our period. And I propose to begin by giving a fairly full, though necessarily very imperfect, synopsis of the old philosophy against which the new doctrines reacted and which they superseded. What I shall describe with the name of the ‘old philosophy’ is the theory of the universe which St Thomas had elaborated on the basis of such knowledge of the works of Aristotle as was available to him.\n\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### The Philosophy of Alfred North Whitehead. Library of Living Philosophers, Vol. III. Edited by Paul Arthur Schilpp, Pp. xviii, 745. $4. 1941. (Northwestern University, Evanston and Chicago)\n\n  *   * C. D. Broad\n  * Journal:\n    The Mathematical Gazette / Volume 26 / Issue 272 / December 1942\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:\n    03 November 2016, pp. 223-225\nPrint publication:\n    December 1942\n  *     * Article\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### A Mathematician's Apology. By G. H. Hardy. (London: Cambridge University Press. 1940. Pp. vii + 93. Price 3s. 6d.)\n\n  *   * C. D. Broad\n  * Journal:\n    Philosophy / Volume 16 / Issue 63 / July 1941\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:\n    25 February 2009, pp. 323-326\nPrint publication:\n    July 1941\n  *     * Article\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe books we love and why – Read a Book Day 2021 | Cambridge University Press & Assessment (https://www.cambridge.org/news-and-insights/insights/the-books-we-love-and-why-%E2%80%93-read-a-book-day-2021)\nciteturn26search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; The book I'd recommend is \"A Mathematician's Apology\" by G.H. ... There's another personal link too as Hardy approached Cambridge University Press with his manuscript in 1940, assuming (wrongly) he'd have to pay to publish it.\n\nThe book I'd recommend is \"A Mathematician's Apology\" by G.H. Hardy. It's a book Hardy wrote in his 60s as he was coming to the end of his career as a top mathematician, and it explains the beauty he found in mathematics and why he thought it was worth studying for its own sake rather than just for its applications to practical problems. The book also tells his life story, including his friendship with Ramanujan, an extraordinary mathematician working as a clerk in Madras. He was invited by Hardy make the long voyage to Cambridge where he spent five years before returning to India where he died soon afterwards aged only 32. There's a film of the story with Dev Patel and Jeremy Irons called The Man who Knew Infinity. Hardy's book changed my life; it helped me understand the magic of mathematics and was central to why I studied it at university. It's also an entertaining read full of good stories and doesn't require any knowledge of mathematics to enjoy it either. There's another personal link too as Hardy approached Cambridge University Press with his manuscript in 1940, assuming (wrongly) he'd have to pay to publish it. The Press printed 4,000 copies in its first run and it's still in print with Cambridge eighty years on.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIan and Betty Ballantine Books and Business (https://findingaids.library.columbia.edu/pdf/cul-13511438.pdf)\nciteturn26search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Scope and Contents ... Mathematician's Delight., 1946\nIan and Betty Ballantine Books and Business Records 13511438\nScope and Contents\nFiction.\nbox 16\nChristian Darnton. You and Music., 1946\nScope and Contents\nAmerican Pelicans P3.\nbox 16\nJames Parkes. An Enemy of the People: Antisemitism.,\n1946\nScope and Contents\nAmerican Pelicans P5.\nbox 16\nW. W. Sawyer. Mathematician's Delight., 1946\nScope and Contents\nAmerican Pelicans P8.\nbox 16\nGeorge Kimble and Raymond Bush. The Weather., 1946\nScope and Contents\nAm